<project>
    Universal package core CLI. One place to create, install, and manage packages/apps across languages. Routes to the native package managers - never replaces them.

    
    - Project: universal-package-core
    - Binary: `upc`
    <goal>
        - Create easily installable apps and packages (installable by this tool)
        - Manage packages in other projects with this tool
    </goal>
    <architecture>
        - CLI only
        - Routes + sets up. Native package managers own manifests, lockfiles, versions, sync, upgrades, and where to look for things.
        - If something required is missing (a package manager, runtime): ask the user if they want it installed, then proceed.
        - Isolation rule: language code lives in ONE package per language. Outside the `python` package, nothing mentions Python. Same for project types: outside the `app` package, nothing app-specific. Same for package managers.
        - Core defines interfaces/base types (`PackageFile`, `Module`, etc.). Each language/PM/project type implements them in its own package only. Core never imports a concrete implementation.
        - Adding a language = adding one package. Zero edits elsewhere (besides registration).
    </architecture>
    <stack>
        - Go for the CLI. Templates are in their own languages.
        - Go has no abstract classes: use interfaces + composition + embedded base structs.
        <detail name="cli-commands">
            - `upc init` - detect existing project and configure, or guided walkthrough for a new one
            - `upc new {type}` - create app/package/script (pick language + PM)
            - `upc add|remove|list|install` - package ops, routed to the right PM
            - `upc update|outdated|audit` - upgrades and checks, routed
            - `upc run {script}` - unified script running (fills gaps like pip)
            - `upc build|test|lint` - unified, routed or filled in
            - `upc up|down` - spin project up/down (like `npm run dev`, `docker compose up`). Wraps existing scripts; creates them if none exist.
            - `upc env list|use|show` - environments (local/dev/test/prod)
            - `upc config get|set|validate` - config, with compatibility checks
            - `upc workspace` - monorepo targeting
            - `upc doctor` - detect missing tools, drift between upc and PM, offer fixes
            - `upc publish` - build + hand off to the PM's publish
            - `upc completion|version`
            - Global flags: `--dry-run`, `--json`, `--yes`, `--verbose`, `--project {path}`, `--global`
            - Non-interactive mode for CI.
        </detail>
    </stack>
    <development>
        - Documentation always updated
          - Architecture decisions go in an ADR in `docs/adrs`
          - ARCHITECTURE.md and CHANGELOG.md updated with every change/progress
        - SOLID and OOP followed strictly (as far as Go allows)
        - Modularity is the top priority - interfaces/abstraction everywhere so new implementations drop in
        - Configurability prioritized - many ways to manage a project, none should cause issues
        - No duplicate/overlapping code - shared behavior goes in shared base/embedded types
        - Logging: structured and liberal. Log decisions, routed commands, failures, and fallbacks.
    </development>
    <testing>
        - Everything is tested - every command, module, language, PM, and project type
        - Tests updated with every change
    </testing>
    <storage>
        - Filesystem only
        - Two scopes: per-project and global
        - Project wins over global
        - Global: `~/.universal-package-core/`
        - Project: `.universal-package-core/` (hidden, in project root)
        - Create the directories when needed
    </storage>
    <requirements name="portability">
        - Drop into an existing project with no disruption or rework
        - Works on any OS
        - No unnecessary disk space
        - Works with package managers, not instead of them
        - Enhances project management, never replaces it
        - Distribution: Homebrew only for now
    </requirements>
    <requirements name="environments">
        - Recognize and manage local/dev/test/prod etc.
        - Environment model is ours to decide: e.g. `.env.local`-style files, or env files mapped in the hidden project config. Decide during build, record in an ADR.
        - Projects get a proper virtual environment (via the PM's own mechanism)
        - Env vars and secrets: not our concern. No handling.
        - Spin up/down: `upc up|down` wraps things like `npm run dev` and `docker compose up`
    </requirements>
    <requirements name="compatible-stacks">
        - Python: `uv` preferred, also `poetry` and `pip`
        - Node (TypeScript): `npm`, `yarn`, others as relevant
        - Fill capability gaps (e.g. `run`/`build` for plain pip)
        - Future languages plug in the same way
        <detail name="parity">
            - Analyze what each PM can and can't do natively.
            - Define a capability set in core. Every PM implements each capability: natively routed, or filled in by us.
            - Adding a new capability = add to the core set. Each PM/language then adds its own implementation easily (missing ones fail loudly, not silently).
        </detail>
    </requirements>
    <requirements name="package-management">
        - Use the native PM for everything: manifests, lockfiles, versions, sync, upgrades
        - Read the PM's live state; don't cache a competing copy
        - Add/remove/list/install etc. route to the right PM
        - Multiple PMs at once, globally or per project
        - Monorepos: figure it out during build. Multiple apps in one folder must be easy.
        - Missing tool: ask to install, then continue
    </requirements>
    <requirements name="configuration">
        - Set up an existing project via the CLI, auto-detecting config
        - Or guided walkthrough for a new project
        - Everything configurable
        - PM switching: allowed within the same language (e.g. uv to pip). Cross-language (e.g. uv to npm) is refused - not guaranteed, not touched.
        - CLI updates config and rejects incompatible settings
    </requirements>
    <requirements name="projects">
        - Project types: app, package, script (top-level). Others (CLI, library) map into these.
        - Any language/PM can define its own types if they map to the three
        - Init leaves the project ready to go
        - Templates: location/format decided as we build
    </requirements>
    <requirements name="modularity">
        - Everything configurable or per-stack gets its own module
        - e.g. "package" is its own project type with its own package: config, capabilities, templates
        - Anything with many-types-interacting-with-many-types follows the same rule
    </requirements>
    <requirements name="observability">
        - Structured logging everywhere
        - Log every routed command, decision, and error
        - Failed ops report clearly and don't leave a half-done state where avoidable
    </requirements>
    <decisions>
        - Stack order: Python (`uv`) first, then Node (`npm`), then the other PMs
        - Project type order: `script`, then `package`, then `app`
    </decisions>
    <acceptance>
        - Spin up any project type with any stack
        - Install and manage packages how I need
        - Spin a project up/down locally in different environments
        - Environment never out of line between PM and upc
    </acceptance>

</project>
